## What happened

Starting with **.NET 11**, `DSA.Create()` throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException` on macOS**. Apple removed DSA support from its Security/CryptoKit frameworks, and .NET followed suit by dropping the macOS DSA implementation entirely. It still works on Linux (via OpenSSL) and Windows (via CNG).

## How to fix it

You have a few options, depending on your constraints:

### 1. Use OpenSSL on macOS via a shim (short-term workaround)
Install OpenSSL on macOS (e.g. `brew install openssl`) and P/Invoke into it, or use a NuGet package like **Portable.BouncyCastle** to verify DSA signatures in managed code on all platforms:

```csharp
// Example with BouncyCastle
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

var signer = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA256withDSA");
signer.Init(forSigning: false, publicKey);
signer.BlockUpdate(data, 0, data.Length);
bool valid = signer.VerifySignature(signature);
```

### 2. Conditional platform logic (if you still target Linux/Windows with built-in DSA)
```csharp
if (OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
{
    // Use BouncyCastle or OpenSSL interop
}
else
{
    using var dsa = DSA.Create();
    // ...
}
```

### 3. Migrate away from DSA entirely (recommended long-term)
DSA is a **legacy algorithm**. If you control the signature format, migrate to **ECDSA** or **Ed25519** (supported natively on all platforms in modern .NET):

```csharp
using var ecdsa = ECDsa.Create(ECCurve.NamedCurves.nistP256);
```

This requires re-signing existing data but eliminates the platform gap permanently.

### Summary

| Option | Effort | Scope |
|---|---|---|
| BouncyCastle shim | Low | Keeps verifying legacy sigs |
| Platform branch | Medium | Mixed approach |
| Migrate to ECDSA/Ed25519 | High | Permanent fix, no legacy debt |

If you only need to **verify** existing signatures (not create new ones), option 1 with BouncyCastle is the lowest-friction path.
